Traditional recycling models treat end-of-life products as waste, creating pure costs that manufacturers externalize through EPR programs. Secondary material markets create conditions where recovered materials become revenue-generating assets. Capturing secondary material value internalizes end-of-life economics, incentivizing design for material purity and recovery efficiency.

Secondary material markets enable manufacturers to reduce virgin material consumption while potentially improving material cost economics. Recovered materials with established market value attract recycler investment in specialized equipment and processes. Secondary material revenue streams create business incentives for product design optimizing material recovery.